<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abstract:</span> A graph <math id="p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> is called
<math id="p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable if
for any total list assignment <math id="p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
which assigns to each vertex <math id="p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> a set <math id="p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(v)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> real numbers,
and assigns to each edge <math id="p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> a set <math id="p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(e)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> real numbers,
there is a mapping <math id="p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(y)\in L(y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(y)\in L(y)</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\in V\cup E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\in V\cup E</annotation></semantics></math>
and for any two adjacent vertices <math id="p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{e\in E(v)}f(e)+f(v)\neq\sum_{e\in E(v^{\prime})}f(e)+f(v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≠</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{e\in E(v)}f(e)+f(v)\neq\sum_{e\in E(v^{\prime})}f(e)+f(v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(x)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of incident edges of a vertex <math id="p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
In this paper, we characterize a sufficient condition on
<math id="p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable of graphs.
We show that every connected <math id="p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph is both <math id="p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable and <math id="p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable if <math id="p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph denotes the graph with <math id="p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices and <math id="p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> edges.
Furthermore, we prove that some graphs obtained by
some graph operations are <math id="p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Total-weighting; List assignment; Permanent index; Line graph; Graph operation</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V(G),E(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V(G),E(G))</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph with <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. The number of edges in <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(G)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> for short. We also call <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as an <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graph</em>.
For a subgraph <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-E(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-E(H)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the subgraph obtained from <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by deleting the edges of <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.
A <em id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">matching</em> in a graph is a set of non-loop edges with no common endvertices, and an endvertex in an edge of a matching is said to be <em id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">saturated</em> by the matching. A <em id="S1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">perfect matching</em> in a graph is a matching that saturates every vertex.
The number of perfect matchings of <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For convenience, a path, a cycle and a complete graph with <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices are denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total-weighting</em> of a graph <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
which assigns to each vertex and each edge a real number as its weight.
For a total-weighting <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>,
we use <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(v)=f(v)+\sum_{e\in E(v)}f(e)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(v)=f(v)+\sum_{e\in E(v)}f(e)</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the weight of a vertex <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(v)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of edges incident with <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(u)\neq s(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(u)\neq s(v)</annotation></semantics></math> for any two adjacent vertices <math id="S1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, then we call the total-weighting <math id="S1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The list version of total-weighting of graphs was introduced independently by Przybyło and Woźniak <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and Wong and Zhu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Let <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi:V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{N}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℕ</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi:V\cup E\rightarrow\mathbb{N}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math>. A <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">list assignment</em> of a graph <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> which assigns to <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in V\cup E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in V\cup E</annotation></semantics></math> a set <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(z)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(z)</annotation></semantics></math> real numbers. Given a total list assignment <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, a proper <math id="S1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-total weighting is a <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper total
weighting</em> <math id="S1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(z)\in L(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(z)\in L(z)</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in V\cup E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in V\cup E</annotation></semantics></math>. We say <math id="S1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total weight</em> <math id="S1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">choosable</em> (<math id="S1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">choosable</em> for short) if for any <math id="S1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-list assignment <math id="S1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a proper <math id="S1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-total weighting
of <math id="S1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We say <math id="S1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">total weight</em> <math id="S1.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">choosable</em> (<math id="S1.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable for short) if <math id="S1.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math>-total weight choosable, where <math id="S1.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(v)=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(v)=k</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi(e)=k^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi(e)=k^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e\in E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e\in E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Wong and Zhu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> proposed two Conjectures as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Every graph with no isolated edges is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Every graph is <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">permanent</em> of an <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> real matrix <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=[a_{ij}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=[a_{ij}]</annotation></semantics></math>,
with <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i,j\in\{1,2,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A)=\sum_{\sigma}\prod_{i=1}^{m}a_{i\sigma(i)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>σ</mi></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A)=\sum_{\sigma}\prod_{i=1}^{m}a_{i\sigma(i)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the summation takes over all permutations <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> of
<math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,m\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,m\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">permanent index</span> of a matrix <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A)</annotation></semantics></math>,
is the minimum integer <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> such that
there exists a matrix <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A^{\prime})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A^{\prime})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
each column of <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a column of <math id="S1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
and each column of <math id="S1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> at most <math id="S1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> times.
Let <math id="S1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be a matrix
with rows indexed by the edges of <math id="S1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
and columns indexed by the vertices and edges of <math id="S1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,
where if <math id="S1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(u,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(u,v)</annotation></semantics></math>(oriented from <math id="S1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>), then</p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{G}[e,y]=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if $y=v$, or $y\neq e$ is an edge incident to $v$},\\
-1&amp;\text{if $y=u$, or $y\neq e$ is an edge incident to $u$},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\\
\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mtext>, or </mtext><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mtext> is an edge incident to </mtext><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mtext>, or </mtext><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mtext> is an edge incident to </mtext><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mtext>otherwise</mtext><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{G}[e,y]=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if $y=v$, or $y\neq e$ is an edge incident to $v$},\\
-1&amp;\text{if $y=u$, or $y\neq e$ is an edge incident to $u$},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}.\\
\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">and let <math id="S1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> be the submatrix of <math id="S1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
with those columns of <math id="S1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> indexed by edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">An <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">index function</span> of <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a mapping <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>,
and it assigns to every vertex or edge <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
a non-negative integer <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{y\in V(G)\cup E(G)}\eta(z)=|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{y\in V(G)\cup E(G)}\eta(z)=|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>,
then the index function <math id="S1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">valid</span>.
For an index function <math id="S1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{(\eta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{(\eta)}</annotation></semantics></math> the matrix,
each of its column is a column of <math id="S1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and each column <math id="S1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}(z)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> can appear up to <math id="S1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(z)</annotation></semantics></math> times in <math id="S1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{(\eta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{(\eta)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> that <math id="S1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable if <math id="S1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S1.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable if <math id="S1.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Bartnicki et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and Wong et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> proposed two Conjectures independently
as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
For any graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with no isolated edges, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">]</span></cite><span id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
For any graph <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The above two conjectures have received a lot of attention.
However, they have not been solved yet,
which can only be proved to be true for some special graphs.
Recently, it was proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>
that every graph with no isolated edges is <math id="S1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,5)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
Some special graphs are shown to be <math id="S1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable,
such as trees, complete graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>,
subcubic graphs, 2-trees, Halin graphs, grids <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.
Some special graphs are shown to be <math id="S1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable,
such as complete graphs, complete bipartite graphs,
trees without <math id="S1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>,
Cartesian product of an even number of even cycles,
of <math id="S1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and an even cycle, of two paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Wong and Zhu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>
showed that if a graph is <math id="S1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable
then it is <math id="S1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k+1,k^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k+1,k^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable and <math id="S1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k,k^{\prime}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k,k^{\prime}+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
Hence there is a natural problem as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_problem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Characterizing graphs that are <math id="S1.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">In response to the above problem,
some results have been obtained.
Wong et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> proved that complete bipartite graphs without <math id="S1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
are <math id="S1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable;
Chang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> proved that a tree with even number of edges is <math id="S1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we focus on Problem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Problem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> and Conjectures <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem3" title="Conjecture 1.3. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a> and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem4" title="Conjecture 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>, we show that some graphs are <math id="S1.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable
as well as <math id="S1.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
The remainder of this paper is organized as follows.
In Section 2, we determine a sufficient condition for a graph to be <math id="S1.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable. As applications, we show that an <math id="S1.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph is <math id="S1.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable when <math id="S1.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Section 3, we prove that all <math id="S1.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graphs are <math id="S1.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable
as well as <math id="S1.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable, where <math id="S1.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p12.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
In the final section, we prove that some graphs under
some graph operations are <math id="S1.p12.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>A solution to Problem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Problem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will characterize a sufficient condition to answer Problem <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem5" title="Problem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a>.
Chang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> gave an important result on <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable of graphs as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>)
If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(B_{G})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(B_{G})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A Sachs graph is a simple graph such that each component is regular and has degree 1 or 2.
In other words the components are single edges and cycles.
Merris et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>
gave a formula for calculating the permanent of any graph <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm per}(A(G))=|(-1)^{n}\sum_{H}2^{k(H)}|," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>H</mi></munder></mstyle><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm per}(A(G))=|(-1)^{n}\sum_{H}2^{k(H)}|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where the summation takes over all Sachs subgraphs <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(H)</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of cycles in <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected graph with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> edges. If the number of perfect matchings in the line graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Replace <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> and the obtained matrix is just the adjacent matrix <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(L(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(L(G))</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be seen that <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(B_{G})\equiv{\rm per}(A(L(G)))({\rm mod}2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(B_{G})\equiv{\rm per}(A(L(G)))({\rm mod}2)</annotation></semantics></math>. According to formula <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1)</annotation></semantics></math>, we get that</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm per}(A(L(G)))=|(-1)^{m}\sum_{H}2^{k(H)}|=M(L(G))+\sum_{H^{\prime}}2^{k(H^{\prime})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>H</mi></munder></mstyle><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></munder></mstyle><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm per}(A(L(G)))=|(-1)^{m}\sum_{H}2^{k(H)}|=M(L(G))+\sum_{H^{\prime}}2^{k(H^{\prime})},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Sachs subgraphs of <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> vertices containing cycles of line graph <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm per}(A(L(G)))\equiv M(L(G))({\rm mod}~2)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm per}(A(L(G)))\equiv M(L(G))({\rm mod}~2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Furthermore,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\rm per}(B_{G})\equiv M(L(G))({\rm mod}~2)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\rm per}(B_{G})\equiv M(L(G))({\rm mod}~2).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 2.1. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> and the above equation, <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable if <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(G))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As applications of Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, we will show that some <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graphs are <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable when <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math> as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Obviously, a connected <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph is a tree when <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Chang et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> proved that a tree with even number of edges is <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
According to Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>,
we can give a new proof. To achieve it,
we first introduce some lemmas as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">For any graph <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,
let <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of components of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
which have an even number of edges.
If <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a forest, <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(G)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math> have the same parity.
Thus, if <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree and <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
then <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p(G-v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(G-v)</annotation></semantics></math> is even for all <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any non-negative <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, denote by <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2k)!!=\frac{(2k)!}{k!\times 2^{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!!</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2k)!!=\frac{(2k)!}{k!\times 2^{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> be a tree with
<math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(T))=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(T-v_{i})!!." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!!</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(T))=\prod_{i=1}^{n}p(T-v_{i})!!.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{(2k)!}{k!\times 2^{k}}=(2k-1)\times(2k-3)\times\ldots\times 3\times 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">!</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{(2k)!}{k!\times 2^{k}}=(2k-1)\times(2k-3)\times\ldots\times 3\times 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-negative integer.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>, Lemmas <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>,
we can get a result as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>)
If <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree with even number of edges.
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we give a recursive expression for <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(G))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> be any edge of <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with endvertices <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(u,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(u,w)</annotation></semantics></math> be the graph obtained from <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-e</annotation></semantics></math>
by adding a new vertex <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and adding a new edge joining <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(v,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(v,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined similarly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.6</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph, and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv</annotation></semantics></math> be an edge of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(G))=M(L(G(u,w)))+M(L(G(v,w)))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(G))=M(L(G(u,w)))+M(L(G(v,w))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">A vertex of degree one is called a <em id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">leaf</em> in a graph. A <em id="S2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unicyclic graph</em> is a connected graph containing exactly one cycle, the cycle denoted by <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Obviously, a connected <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph is unicyclic if and only if <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=m</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of unicyclic graphs with <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices is denoted by <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any graph <math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(C_{l})=\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{l}\}\subseteq V(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(C_{l})=\{v_{1},\ldots,v_{l}\}\subseteq V(U)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as identifying <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with any leaf of each of <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees for <math id="S2.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,l\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,l\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-negative integer.
Denote by <math id="S2.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{0}(\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{0}(\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{1}(\geq 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{1}(\geq 3)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively the number of trees with even number of edges and odd number of edges in the <math id="S2.p9.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees. Let <math id="S2.p9.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=\sum_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=\sum_{i=1}^{l}k_{i}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p9.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of <math id="S2.p9.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p9.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.p9.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be the subset of <math id="S2.p9.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p9.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
We denote the <math id="S2.p9.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> trees with even number of edges as <math id="S2.p9.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{1},T_{2},\ldots,T_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{1},T_{2},\ldots,T_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>,
respectively. As we will consider the number of perfect matchings of line graphs, assume that <math id="S2.p9.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even and all the notation in this paragraph is followed in Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> and Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.7</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathscr{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathscr{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(U))=M(L(T_{1}))+M(L(T_{2}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(U))=M(L(T_{1}))+M(L(T_{2}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the edge incident with <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}=U-E(T_{1}-e_{1})-E(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-E(T_{s}-e_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}=U-E(T_{1}-e_{1})-E(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-E(T_{s}-e_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to the definition of <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(T_{i}-e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(T_{i}-e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> are odd, <math id="S2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(U)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
So
<math id="S2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(U^{\prime})=m(U)-m(T_{1}-e_{1})-m(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-m(T_{s}-e_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(U^{\prime})=m(U)-m(T_{1}-e_{1})-m(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-m(T_{s}-e_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
then <math id="S2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(U^{\prime}))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(U^{\prime}))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, <math id="S2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(T_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(T_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd as <math id="S2.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(T_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(T_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
From the above argument and equation,
we obtain that <math id="S2.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(U))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(U))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. Then <math id="S2.p11.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathscr{U}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathscr{U}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p11.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable
by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.8</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathscr{U}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathscr{U}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(U))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(U))</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(U))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(U))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(T_{1}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(T_{1}))+\ldots+M(L(T_{s}))+M(L(U^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
