<p id="S2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the edge incident with <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}=U-E(T_{1}-e_{1})-E(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-E(T_{s}-e_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}=U-E(T_{1}-e_{1})-E(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-E(T_{s}-e_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv</annotation></semantics></math> denotes any edge of <math id="S2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p12.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}(x,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}(x,w)</annotation></semantics></math> is the graph obtained from <math id="S2.p12.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G-e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G-e</annotation></semantics></math>
by adding a new vertex <math id="S2.p12.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> and adding a new edge <math id="S2.p12.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="wx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">wx</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p12.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\{u,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\{u,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the above definition of <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{U}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{U}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(T_{i}-e_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(T_{i}-e_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(U)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(U)</annotation></semantics></math> are even.
So, <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(U^{\prime})=m(U)-m(T_{1}-e_{1})-m(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-m(T_{s}-e_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(U^{\prime})=m(U)-m(T_{1}-e_{1})-m(T_{2}-e_{2})-\ldots-m(T_{s}-e_{s})</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
Hence <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(U^{\prime}(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(U^{\prime}(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(U^{\prime}(v,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(U^{\prime}(v,w))</annotation></semantics></math> are even, and <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U^{\prime}(u,w),U^{\prime}(v,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U^{\prime}(u,w),U^{\prime}(v,w)</annotation></semantics></math> are trees.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(U^{\prime}(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(U^{\prime}(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(U^{\prime}(v,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>U</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(U^{\prime}(v,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> are odd.
Since <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(T_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(T_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> is even, by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem5" title="Theorem 2.5. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.5</span></a>, we have that <math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(T_{i}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(T_{i}))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.
From the above argument and equation,
we obtain that <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(U))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(U))</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">A connected <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graph containing two or three cycles is called a <em id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bicyclic graph</em> if <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all bicyclic graphs with <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices.
By the structure of bicyclic graphs, it is known that <math id="S2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>
consists of three types of graphs:
the first type, denoted by <math id="S2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>,
is the set of graphs each of which contains <math id="S2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>
as a vertex-induced subgraph;
the second type, denoted by <math id="S2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>,
is the set of graphs each of which contains <math id="S2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>
as a vertex-induced subgraph;
the third type, denoted by <math id="S2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>,
is the set of graphs each of which contains <math id="S2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>
as a vertex-induced subgraph (see Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Obviously, <math id="S2.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}=\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)\cup\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)\cup\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}=\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)\cup\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)\cup\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
