<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the induced cycles of any bicyclic graph in <math id="S2.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. For any graph <math id="S2.p15.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.p15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(B_{1}(p,q))=\{v_{0},v_{1},\ldots,v_{p+q-1}\}\subseteq V(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(B_{1}(p,q))=\{v_{0},v_{1},\ldots,v_{p+q-1}\}\subseteq V(B)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as identifying <math id="S2.p15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with any leaf of each of <math id="S2.p15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees for <math id="S2.p15.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\in\{1,\ldots,p+q-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\in\{1,\ldots,p+q-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S2.p15.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-negative integer. Denote by <math id="S2.p15.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{0}(\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{0}(\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p15.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{1}(\geq 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{1}(\geq 3)</annotation></semantics></math> respectively the number of trees with even number of edges and odd number of edges in the <math id="S2.p15.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees. Let <math id="S2.p15.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}=\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}=\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p15.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p15.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S2.p15.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{1}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{1}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> such that each graph in <math id="S2.p15.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> contains even number of edges and <math id="S2.p15.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd or even. As we will consider the number of perfect matchings of line graphs, assume that <math id="S2.p15.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and all the notation in this paragraph and Figure 1 is followed in Lemmas <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>, <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>, <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 2.11. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a>, <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 2.12. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem13" title="Theorem 2.13. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.13</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.9</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bicyclic graph. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(v_{1},w)))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(v_{1},w))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w),B(v_{1},w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w),B(v_{1},w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
According to the definition of <math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
Then the number of edges of
<math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(v_{1},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(v_{1},w)</annotation></semantics></math> are even.
Set <math id="S2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=x</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=y</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(v_{1},w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(v_{1},w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text">Without loss of generality,
assume <math id="S2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd or even,
where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be obtained from <math id="S2.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> by deleting the edges in
<math id="S2.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and the trees hanging on it.
Because <math id="S2.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is either an even or an odd number,
so we should consider two cases as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1.<span id="S2.p19.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is even,
then <math id="S2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.
According to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, <math id="S2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(v_{1},w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(v_{1},w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
It follows that <math id="S2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation. Therefore, <math id="S2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S2.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is even, then <math id="S2.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S2.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even according to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>. By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, <math id="S2.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(v_{1},w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(v_{1},w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. Therefore, <math id="S2.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S2.p22.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.p23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
then <math id="S2.p23.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p23.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.p23.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even according to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, <math id="S2.p23.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(v_{1},w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(v_{1},w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. Then <math id="S2.p23.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p23.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p23.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S2.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
then <math id="S2.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p24.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.p24.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>.
According to Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>, <math id="S2.p24.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(v_{1},w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(v_{1},w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Therefore, <math id="S2.p24.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p24.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p24.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p25.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the induced cycles of any bicyclic graph in <math id="S2.p25.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S2.p25.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{2}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{2}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>
be the set of all graphs obtained
by identifying every vertex <math id="S2.p25.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p25.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(p,q,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(p,q,2)</annotation></semantics></math> with
any leaf of each of the <math id="S2.p25.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees,
where <math id="S2.p25.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(the number of trees with even number of edges <math id="S2.p25.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math> denoted by <math id="S2.p25.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the number of trees with odd number of edges <math id="S2.p25.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\geq 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\geq 3)</annotation></semantics></math> denoted by <math id="S2.p25.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)
and such that <math id="S2.p25.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p25.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S2.p25.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is even or <math id="S2.p25.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p25.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.
Because we need to consider the number of perfect matchings of line graph,
<math id="S2.p25.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be odd.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.10</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bicyclic graph. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(v^{\prime},w)))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(v^{\prime},w))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text">Obviously, <math id="S2.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w),B(v^{\prime},w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w),B(v^{\prime},w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the definition of <math id="S2.p27.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p27.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
We consider two cases as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S2.p28.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S2.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(v^{\prime},w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(v^{\prime},w))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the above definition, we have <math id="S2.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd
and the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(v^{\prime},w)))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(v^{\prime},w)))=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>. It follows that <math id="S2.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S2.p30.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.p30.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(v^{\prime},w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(v^{\prime},w))</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the above definition, we have <math id="S2.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd
and the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p31.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p31.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p31.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(v^{\prime},w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(v^{\prime},w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>. It follows that <math id="S2.p31.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p31.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p31.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p32.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the induced cycle of any bicyclic graph in <math id="S2.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p32.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{3}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{3}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{2}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>
be the set of all graphs obtained
by identifying every vertex <math id="S2.p32.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p32.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(p,q,r)(r&gt;2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(p,q,r)(r&gt;2)</annotation></semantics></math> and
any leaf of each of the <math id="S2.p32.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees,
where <math id="S2.p32.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(the number of trees with even number of edges <math id="S2.p32.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p32.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the number of trees with odd number of edges <math id="S2.p32.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\geq 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\geq 3)</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p32.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)
and <math id="S2.p32.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>,
where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p32.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>
if <math id="S2.p32.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is even, <math id="S2.p32.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is odd or
<math id="S2.p32.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.p32.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, <math id="S2.p32.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
Because we need to consider the number of perfect matches of line graph,
<math id="S2.p32.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be odd.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.11</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bicyclic graph. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uz" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uz</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(z,w)))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(z,w))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">Obviously, <math id="S2.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w),B(z,w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w),B(z,w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
According to the definition of <math id="S2.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p34.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
We consider two cases as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S2.p35.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is even and <math id="S2.p35.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(z,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(z,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the above definition, we have <math id="S2.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd,
where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p36.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(z,w)))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(z,w)))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, <math id="S2.p36.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. It follows that <math id="S2.p36.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p36.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p36.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S2.p37.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(u,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(u,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S2.p37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B(z,w))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B(z,w))</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the above definition, we have <math id="S2.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is even,
where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p38.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
By Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, <math id="S2.p38.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(z,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(z,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. It follows that <math id="S2.p38.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p38.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p38.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p39.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{4}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{4}\subset\mathscr{B}_{n}^{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>
be the set of all graphs obtained
by identifying every vertex <math id="S2.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math> with
any leaf of each of the <math id="S2.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> trees,
where <math id="S2.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}=k_{i}^{0}+k_{i}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(the number of trees with even number of edges <math id="S2.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the number of trees with odd number of edges <math id="S2.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\geq 3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\geq 3)</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S2.p39.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{i}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{i}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>)
and <math id="S2.p39.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>k</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{i}k_{i}^{0}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd or even,
where the summation takes over all vertices of <math id="S2.p39.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p39.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p39.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because we shall think about the number of perfect matchings of line graph,
<math id="S2.p39.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed to be odd.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.12</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> be a bicyclic graph. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem12.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p40" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uy</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 2.6. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.6</span></a>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(y,w)))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M(L(B))=M(L(B(u,w)))+M(L(B(y,w))).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p41.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p41.1.1" class="ltx_text">Clearly, <math id="S2.p41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w),B(y,w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w),B(y,w)\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the definition of <math id="S2.p41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p41.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B)</annotation></semantics></math> is even.
Then the number of edges of <math id="S2.p41.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p41.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(y,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(y,w)</annotation></semantics></math> are both even.
Set <math id="S2.p41.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p41.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(u,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(u,w)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p41.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s=y^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=y^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p41.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(y,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(y,w)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p42" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S2.p42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a subgraph of <math id="S2.p42.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
induced by <math id="S2.p42.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, edges <math id="S2.p42.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uy,vx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uy,vx</annotation></semantics></math> and the trees hanging
on all vertices of <math id="S2.p42.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider two cases as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p43" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p43.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S2.p43.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is even.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p44.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p44.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then <math id="S2.p44.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.p44.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p44.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> and <math id="S2.p44.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(y,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(y,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>. It follows that <math id="S2.p44.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p44.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p44.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p45" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p45.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is even, then <math id="S2.p45.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.p45.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.p45.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a> and <math id="S2.p45.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(y,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(y,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>. It follows that <math id="S2.p45.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation <math id="S2.p45.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p45.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p45.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p46.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p46.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S2.p46.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S2.p46.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(B^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(B^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is odd.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p47.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p47.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p47.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then <math id="S2.p47.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S2.p47.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.p47.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>.
According to Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a>, we have <math id="S2.p47.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(y,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(y,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd. It follows that <math id="S2.p47.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation <math id="S2.p47.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(7)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(7)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p47.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p47.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p48.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p48.1.1" class="ltx_text">If <math id="S2.p48.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is even,then <math id="S2.p48.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}=s_{1}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}=s_{1}+1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p48.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{\prime}=s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{\prime}=s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, <math id="S2.p48.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(u,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(u,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 2.7. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.7</span></a> and
<math id="S2.p48.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B(y,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B(y,w)))</annotation></semantics></math> is even by Lemma <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem8" title="Lemma 2.8. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.8</span></a>.
It follows that <math id="S2.p48.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M(L(B))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M(L(B))</annotation></semantics></math> is odd by the above equation and <math id="S2.p48.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p48.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p49.1" class="ltx_p">According to Lemmas <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem9" title="Lemma 2.9. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.9</span></a>,<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem10" title="Lemma 2.10. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.10</span></a>,<a href="#S2.Thmtheorem11" title="Lemma 2.11. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.11</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem12" title="Lemma 2.12. ‣ 2 A solution to Problem ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.12</span></a>,
we obtain the following result in this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.13</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}\cup\mathscr{B}_{2}\cup\mathscr{B}_{3}\cup\mathscr{B}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{1}\cup\mathscr{B}_{2}\cup\mathscr{B}_{3}\cup\mathscr{B}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>
be a bicyclic graph.
Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.Thmtheorem13.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Total weight choosability of <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graphs when <math id="S3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will show that all <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graphs are
<math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable, where <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n+1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Obviously, <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graphs are unicyclic graphs when <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,m)</annotation></semantics></math>-graphs are bicyclic graphs when <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=n+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=n+1</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">sink</em> in a digraph is a vertex of outdegree zero. Before the proof of main theorems,
we present some lemmas as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>)
Let an index function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> be non-singular
if there is a valid index function <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}\leq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}\leq\eta</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A_{G}(\eta^{\prime}))\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A_{G}(\eta^{\prime}))\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Suppose <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph, <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is an index function of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(e)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(e)=1</annotation></semantics></math> for all edges <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math>,
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a subset of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G-E[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G-E[X]</annotation></semantics></math> be obtained from <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
by deleting edges in <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be an acyclic orientation of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
in which each vertex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in X</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink.
Assume that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sub-digraph of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>
such that for all <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(D)</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\eta(v)+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\eta(v)+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the index function defined as <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}(e)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}(e)=1</annotation></semantics></math>
for every edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[X]</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}(v)=\eta(v)+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}(v)=\eta(v)+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in X</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G[X]</annotation></semantics></math>,
then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>)
Suppose <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from a graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding one vertex <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>
and one edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a vertex of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree with at least two edges, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{T})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{T})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hanging edge</em> of a graph <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an edge <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(u)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(u)=2</annotation></semantics></math> or 3.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph containing a hanging edge <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=uv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=uv</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}=G-\{u,v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}=G-\{u,v\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be obtained from a graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by adding two new vertices <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math>
and two new edges <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}=uv,e_{2}=uw" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=uv,e_{2}=uw</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq{\rm pind}(B_{G})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq{\rm pind}(B_{G})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(V,E)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(V,E)</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph such that <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> be a nonempty subset of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Denote by <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> the graph obtained by adding two new vertices <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u,v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u,v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
and joining them to each vertex of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{F})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{F})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">thread</em> in a graph <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a path <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>
in <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{G}(v_{i})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{G}(v_{i})=2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2,3,\ldots,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2,3,\ldots,k-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The vertices <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> need not to be distinct.
If we need to specify the two end vertices of a thread,
then we say <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(v_{1}-v_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(v_{1}-v_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>-thread.
By <em id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deleting a thread</em> <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(v_{1},v_{2},\ldots,v_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>,
we mean deleting the vertices <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2},v_{3},\ldots,v_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2},v_{3},\ldots,v_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(and hence edges incident to them). The <em id="S3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">length</em> of a thread is the number of edges in it. The notations defined in section 2 are followed in this section.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be obtained from a graph <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
by deleting a thread of length 4.
If <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G^{\prime}})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{G})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{U})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{U})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, it is sufficient to prove this Theorem holds for the unique cycle <math id="S3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">First we construct an acyclic orientation of <math id="S3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
orient the edges <math id="S3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}v_{i+1}(i=1,2,\ldots,l-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}v_{i+1}(i=1,2,\ldots,l-1)</annotation></semantics></math>
from <math id="S3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and orient the edge <math id="S3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}v_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}v_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The resulting digraph is denoted by <math id="S3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink vertex in <math id="S3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> obviously. Let <math id="S3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sub-digraph of <math id="S3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of the edge <math id="S3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}v_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}v_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant function,
<math id="S3.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{v_{l}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{v_{l}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}(v_{l})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}(v_{l})=0</annotation></semantics></math> be an index function of <math id="S3.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because there exist a valid index function <math id="S3.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A_{U[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A_{U[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
To prove that <math id="S3.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{U})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{U})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>,
is suffices, by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
to show that for each vertex <math id="S3.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We show that every vertex <math id="S3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the above equation and consider three cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S3.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=d_{D}^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=d_{D}^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>=0, <math id="S3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S3.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{i}(i=2,3,\ldots,l-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{i}(i=2,3,\ldots,l-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3:<span id="S3.p11.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then
<math id="S3.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D}^{-}(v)=2,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D}^{-}(v)=2,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.9</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\in\mathscr{U}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\in\mathscr{U}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem9.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">We consider three cases as follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p14.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p14.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S3.p14.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p15.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p15.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> by Wong and Zhu in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.
Hence <math id="S3.p15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p15.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S3.p16.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph obtained
by identifying vertex <math id="S3.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with
the center of a star <math id="S3.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{1,s_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{1,s_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq l</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p17.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p17.1.1" class="ltx_text">First consider the case <math id="S3.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> according to the direct calculation.
Hence <math id="S3.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
Based on the above result and Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>, <math id="S3.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p17.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable if <math id="S3.p17.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. The proof of the case <math id="S3.p17.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2</annotation></semantics></math>
is similar to <math id="S3.p17.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1</annotation></semantics></math> and is thus omitted.
By repeating the above process,
we can prove the above Theorem holds for <math id="S3.p17.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=3,4,\ldots,l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=3,4,\ldots,l</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3:<span id="S3.p18.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph obtained
by identifying vertex <math id="S3.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p18.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> with
a vertex of a tree <math id="S3.p18.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{s_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{s_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p18.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq l</annotation></semantics></math>
and at least one <math id="S3.p18.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{s_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{s_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not a star.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p19.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p19.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove the Theorem by induction on <math id="S3.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}=|\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{l}E(T_{s_{i}})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}=|\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{l}E(T_{s_{i}})|</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S3.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p19.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math> according to Case <math id="S3.p19.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that the above Theorem holds for the number of edges in the hanging trees
less than <math id="S3.p19.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}(\geq 2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mspace style="width:0.389em;" width="0.389em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}(\geq 2)</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider the case that <math id="S3.p19.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{l}E(T_{s_{i}})|=m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>l</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\bigcup\limits_{i=1}^{l}E(T_{s_{i}})|=m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By induction hypothesis, the above Theorem holds for <math id="S3.p19.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m^{\prime}-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m^{\prime}-2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p19.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>U</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(B_{U})\leq 2</annotation></semantics></math>
by Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma 3.5. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Lemma 3.6. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>. Hence <math id="S3.p19.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p19.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,3)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.10</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathscr{B}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℬ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathscr{B}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
we only need to prove this Theorem holds for <math id="S3.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(p,q,\\
r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(p,q,\\
r)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math>.
We consider three cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p21" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p21.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p21.1.1" class="ltx_text">First we construct an acyclic orientation of <math id="S3.p21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}(p,q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}(p,q)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
For <math id="S3.p21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, except for the clockwise orientation of edge <math id="S3.p21.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}u</annotation></semantics></math>,
all the other edges are oriented anticlockwise;
For <math id="S3.p21.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, except for the anticlockwise orientation of edge <math id="S3.p21.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}u</annotation></semantics></math>,
all the other edges are oriented clockwise.
The resulting digraph is denoted by <math id="S3.p21.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p21.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sub-digraph of <math id="S3.p21.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of the edges <math id="S3.p21.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}u,v_{2}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}u,v_{2}u</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is easy to see that <math id="S3.p21.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink of <math id="S3.p21.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p21.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant function, <math id="S3.p21.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{u\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p21.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}(u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}(u)=0</annotation></semantics></math> be an index function of <math id="S3.p21.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because there exist a valid index function <math id="S3.p21.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p21.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A_{B[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A_{B[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p21.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p21.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
To prove that <math id="S3.p21.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.p21.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p21.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>,
is suffices, by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
to show that for each vertex <math id="S3.p21.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p22.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p22.1.1" class="ltx_text">We show that every vertex <math id="S3.p22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies the above equation by considering three cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p23" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p23.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p23.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S3.p23.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=u</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p24.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p24.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=2,d_{D}^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=2,d_{D}^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>=4, <math id="S3.p24.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p24.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p25.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p25.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S3.p25.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{1},v_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{1},v_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p26.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p26.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p26.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p27" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p27.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p27.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3:<span id="S3.p27.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in B\setminus\{u,v_{1},v_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in B\setminus\{u,v_{1},v_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p28" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p28.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p28.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p29.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p29.1.1" class="ltx_text">Secondly, we construct an acyclic orientation of <math id="S3.p29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
For <math id="S3.p29.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, except for the clockwise orientation of edge <math id="S3.p29.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{3}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{3}u</annotation></semantics></math>,
all the other edges are oriented anticlockwise;
For <math id="S3.p29.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, except for the anticlockwise orientation of edge <math id="S3.p29.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{4}v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{4}v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
all the other edges are oriented clockwise;
For <math id="S3.p29.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, orient all edges from right to left.
The resulting digraph is denoted by <math id="S3.p29.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p29.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sub-digraph of <math id="S3.p29.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of edges <math id="S3.p29.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{3}u,v_{4}v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{3}u,v_{4}v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is easy to see that there <math id="S3.p29.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink vertex of <math id="S3.p29.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p29.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant function, <math id="S3.p29.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{u\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p29.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}(u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}(u)=0</annotation></semantics></math> be an index function of <math id="S3.p29.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because there exist a valid index function <math id="S3.p29.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p29.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A_{B[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A_{B[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p29.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p29.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
To prove that <math id="S3.p29.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.p29.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p29.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>,
is suffices, by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
to show that for each vertex <math id="S3.p29.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p30.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p30.1.1" class="ltx_text">We show that every vertex of <math id="S3.p30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
satisfies the above equation by considering four cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p31" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p31.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p31.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 1:<span id="S3.p31.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=u</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p32" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p32.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p32.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D}^{-}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D}^{-}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>=3, <math id="S3.p32.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p32.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p33.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p33.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 2:<span id="S3.p33.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p34.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p34.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D}^{-}(v)=2,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D}^{-}(v)=2,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p35" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 3:<span id="S3.p35.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=v_{3},v_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=v_{3},v_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p36.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p36.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then
<math id="S3.p36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=1\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p37.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p37.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Case 4:<span id="S3.p37.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> <math id="S3.p37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in B\setminus\{u,v^{\prime},v_{3},v_{4}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in B\setminus\{u,v^{\prime},v_{3},v_{4}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p38.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p38.1.1" class="ltx_text">Then <math id="S3.p38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)=0,d_{D}^{-}(v)=1,d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
So <math id="S3.p38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1+2d_{D^{\prime}}^{-}(v)-d_{D}^{-}(v)=0\geq d_{D^{\prime}}^{+}(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p39" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text">Finally, we construct an acyclic orientation of <math id="S3.p39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{3}(p,q,r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{3}(p,q,r)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
For the cycle consisting of <math id="S3.p39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p39.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> and
the edges <math id="S3.p39.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{5}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{5}u</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p39.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{8}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{8}u</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p39.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{6}v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{6}v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p39.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{7}v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>7</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{7}v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
except for the anticlockwise orientation of edges <math id="S3.p39.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{8}u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{8}u</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p39.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{6}v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{6}v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
all the other edges are oriented clockwise;
For <math id="S3.p39.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> and the edges <math id="S3.p39.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="uy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">uy</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p39.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="xv^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">xv^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
orient all edges from top to bottom.
The resulting digraph is denoted by <math id="S3.p39.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.p39.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a sub-digraph of <math id="S3.p39.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of the edges <math id="S3.p39.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{8}u,v_{6}v^{\prime},uy" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>6</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{8}u,v_{6}v^{\prime},uy</annotation></semantics></math>.
It is easy to see that <math id="S3.p39.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is a sink vertex of <math id="S3.p39.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.p39.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\equiv 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\equiv 1</annotation></semantics></math> be a constant function, <math id="S3.p39.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\{u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\{u\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p39.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}(u)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}(u)=0</annotation></semantics></math> be an index function of <math id="S3.p39.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because there exist a valid index function <math id="S3.p39.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime\prime}\leq\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p39.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A_{B[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A_{B[X]}(\eta^{\prime\prime}))\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="S3.p39.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>η</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p39.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B[X]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B[X]</annotation></semantics></math>.
To prove that <math id="S3.p39.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{B})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S3.p39.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> is a non-singular index function of <math id="S3.p39.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>,
is suffices, by Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 3.1. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>,
to show that for each vertex <math id="S3.p39.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
