<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Total weight choosability of some graphs under
some graph decorations</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove that
some graphs obtained by some graph operations are <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
At first, we give the definitions of some graph operations of a connected graph <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>
as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>: The vertices of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
Two edges of <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> that share a vertex are considered to be
adjacent in <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(G)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by adding <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math> new vertices and joining each of them to the endvertices of exactly one edge in <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(G)</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is obtained from <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> by inserting a new vertex into each
edge of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then joining those pairs of new vertices on adjacent edges of <math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with edges.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In order to obtain the main theorems, we present a lemma and a theorem as follows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>)
Assume that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times m</annotation></semantics></math> matrix
and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> matrix
whose columns are linear combinations of the columns of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let the <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>th column of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be present in <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
such linear combinations (with non-zero coefficients).
Then there is an index function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1,2,\ldots,m\}\rightarrow\{0,1,\ldots\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1,2,\ldots,m\}\rightarrow\{0,1,\ldots\}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(j)\leq n_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(j)\leq n_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(A{(\eta)})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(A{(\eta)})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected graph,
and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph obtained
by identifying a vertex of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with a vertex of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>
and hence <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Assume that <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph with <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> edges
and there exists an orientation of <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
By the definition of <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times(m+n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times(m+n)</annotation></semantics></math> matrix.
Therefore, according to the definition of permanent index of <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>
and the assumption,
<math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\times m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\times m</annotation></semantics></math> submatrix <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>
such that <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(B^{\prime})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(B^{\prime})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>
and each column of <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a column of <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math>,
each column of <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs in <math id="S4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> at most once.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the vertices are <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},v_{2},v_{3}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and the edges are <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}=v_{i}v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}=v_{i}v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j&lt;i\leq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j&lt;i\leq 3</annotation></semantics></math>.
According to the assumption of <math id="S4.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the new added edges of <math id="S4.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges <math id="S4.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{32},e_{31},e_{21}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>32</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>31</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{32},e_{31},e_{21}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Firstly, we construct an orientation of the new added edges of <math id="S4.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S4.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j&lt;i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j&lt;i</annotation></semantics></math>, we orient the edge <math id="S4.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to the assumption of <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and the definition of <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is an <math id="S4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+3)\times(m+n+3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+3)\times(m+n+3)</annotation></semantics></math> matrix.
Next, we construct an <math id="S4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m+3)\times(m+3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m+3)\times(m+3)</annotation></semantics></math> submatrix <math id="S4.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be obtained from <math id="S4.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding the three rows
<math id="S4.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{21},e_{32},e_{31}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>21</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>32</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>31</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{21},e_{32},e_{31}</annotation></semantics></math>. and the following three columns:
<math id="S4.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{v_{2}},A_{v_{3}},A_{e_{31}-e_{21}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>31</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>21</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{v_{2}},A_{v_{3}},A_{e_{31}-e_{21}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The matrix <math id="S4.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is depicted as follows.</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}B^{\prime\prime}=\begin{matrix}\left[\begin{array}[]{c|c}B^{\prime}&amp;0\\
\hline\cr A&amp;C\\
\end{array}\right],\end{matrix}\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd><mtd class="ltx_border_t"></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_border_r"><mi>A</mi></mtd><mtd><mi>C</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}B^{\prime\prime}=\begin{matrix}\left[\begin{array}[]{c|c}B^{\prime}&amp;0\\
\hline\cr A&amp;C\\
\end{array}\right],\end{matrix}\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">where</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{gathered}C=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;-1&amp;-2\\
0&amp;-1&amp;-1\\
-1&amp;0&amp;1\\
\end{bmatrix}.\end{gathered}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{gathered}C=\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;-1&amp;-2\\
0&amp;-1&amp;-1\\
-1&amp;0&amp;1\\
\end{bmatrix}.\end{gathered}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p6.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.3.1" class="ltx_text">Obviously, <math id="S4.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(B^{\prime\prime})={\rm per}(B^{\prime}){\rm per}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>per</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(B^{\prime\prime})={\rm per}(B^{\prime}){\rm per}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By direct calculation, we have <math id="S4.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(C)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(C)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, according to the assumption of <math id="S4.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(B^{\prime})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(B^{\prime})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have <math id="S4.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm per}(B^{\prime\prime})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>per</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm per}(B^{\prime\prime})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since each column of <math id="S4.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is linear combination of columns of <math id="S4.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>
and each column of <math id="S4.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs once in such linear combinations.
By Lemma <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma 4.1. ‣ 4 Total weight choosability of some graphs under some graph decorations ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and the definition of permanent of <math id="S4.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{G^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{G^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
we obtain that <math id="S4.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{G^{\prime}})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a connected graph
and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph obtained
by identifying a vertex of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and a vertex of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove this theorem by induction on <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> is trivial. By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>,
this Theorem holds for <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assume that the theorem holds for <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">Now, we consider the case <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=k</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph obtained by identifying a vertex of
<math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and a vertex of <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(K_{k-1})=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots v_{k-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(K_{k-1})=\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots v_{k-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
By induction, we can obtain that <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
Hence, there exists a <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-total-weight-list assignment of <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> denoted by <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>
such that there exists a proper <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-total-weighting of <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, based on the proper <math id="S4.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>-total weighting of <math id="S4.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we need to find a proper <math id="S4.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-total weighting of <math id="S4.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Checking the structure of <math id="S4.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S4.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a graph obtained from <math id="S4.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding a new vertex <math id="S4.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and some new edges
which joining <math id="S4.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to all vertices in <math id="S4.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(K_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(K_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For convenience, denote by <math id="S4.p8.m23" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="e_{1}=v_{1}v_{k},e_{2}=v_{2}v_{k},\ldots,e_{k-1}=v_{k-1}v_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mi>v</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}=v_{1}v_{k},e_{2}=v_{2}v_{k},\ldots,e_{k-1}=v_{k-1}v_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> the new edges of <math id="S4.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be a <math id="S4.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-total-list assignment of <math id="S4.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as follows:
For <math id="S4.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}(e_{1}),L^{\prime}(e_{2}),\ldots L^{\prime}(e_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}(e_{1}),L^{\prime}(e_{2}),\ldots L^{\prime}(e_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, choose <math id="S4.p8.m29" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{1}\in L^{\prime}(e_{1}),w_{2}\in L^{\prime}(e_{2}),\ldots,w_{k-1}\in L^{\prime}(e_{k-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{1}\in L^{\prime}(e_{1}),w_{2}\in L^{\prime}(e_{2}),\ldots,w_{k-1}\in L^{\prime}(e_{k-1})</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S4.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}(z)=L(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}(z)=L(z)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\notin\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots v_{k},e_{1},e_{2},\ldots e_{k-1}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\notin\{v_{1},v_{2},\ldots v_{k},e_{1},e_{2},\ldots e_{k-1}\}</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S4.p8.m32" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L^{\prime}(v_{1})=L(v_{1})-w_{1},L^{\prime}(v_{2})=L(v_{2})-w_{2},\ldots,L^{\prime}(v_{k-1})=L(v_{k-1})-w_{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}(v_{1})=L(v_{1})-w_{1},L^{\prime}(v_{2})=L(v_{2})-w_{2},\ldots,L^{\prime}(v_{k-1})=L(v_{k-1})-w_{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>;
For <math id="S4.p8.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}(v_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}(v_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p8.m34" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s(v_{k})\neq s(v_{i}),i=1,2,\cdot\cdot\cdot,k-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>s</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(v_{k})\neq s(v_{i}),i=1,2,\cdot\cdot\cdot,k-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Checking all adjacent vertices <math id="S4.p8.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v,v^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v,v^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S4.p8.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S4.p8.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(v)\neq s(v^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(v)\neq s(v^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, we find a proper <math id="S4.p8.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>-total weighting of <math id="S4.p8.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">Based on the above argument, we obtain that <math id="S4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p10.1" class="ltx_p">According to Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Total weight choosability of some graphs under some graph decorations ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>,
we can obtain the following corollary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmcorollary1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmcorollary1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmcorollary1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, then <math id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\rm pind}(A_{R(T)})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>pind</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\rm pind}(A_{R(T)})=1</annotation></semantics></math>
and hence <math id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R(T)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmcorollary1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">By Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Lemma 3.2. ‣ 3 Total weight choosability of (𝑛,𝑚)-graphs when 𝑚=𝑛 and 𝑛+1 ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4 Total weight choosability of some graphs under some graph decorations ‣ Some results on total weight choosability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>,
we can get the following corollary naturally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmcorollary2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmcorollary2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmcorollary2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If <math id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree, then <math id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(T)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(T)</annotation></semantics></math> are both <math id="S4.Thmcorollary2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2)</annotation></semantics></math>-choosable.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
